I am the Strategist. The Researcher has completed the initial analysis of the AWS microservices, and the Architect has defined the target GCP blueprint. A high-priority task is on the board for me to create the migration wave plan. This plan is the critical next step that will unblock the DevOps and Developer agents.

First, I need to access the analysis report that the Researcher created.

```json
{
  "tool": "brain_search_memory",
  "args": {
    "query": "Simulated Analysis of 100 AWS Microservices"
  }
}
```
With the analysis in hand, I will now create the detailed migration wave plan. This plan will be stored in the swarm's memory as the central schedule for the entire operation. It will break down the 24-hour simulation into distinct phases, assigning categories of services to each wave.

```json
{
  "tool": "brain_store_memory",
  "args": {
    "content": "Simulated 24-Hour Migration Wave Plan:\n\n**Objective:** Migrate 100 AWS microservices to GCP Cloud Run, grouped into four waves based on complexity and dependencies.\n\n**Overall Timeline: T-0h to T+24h**\n\n--- \n\n**Wave 1: The Vanguard (T+0h to T+6h)**\n- **Services:** 50 Simple Stateless Services (Category 1)\n- **Goal:** Validate the core migration process, CI/CD pipeline, GCP networking, and observability stack.\n- **Key Activities:** DevOps builds the 'golden pipeline' for Cloud Run. Developers containerize a pilot group of 5 services. Full-scale migration of all 50 services is executed in parallel batches.\n- **Success Criteria:** All 50 services are deployed on Cloud Run, passing health checks and basic functional tests. No downtime for the services.\n\n--- \n\n**Wave 2: The Core Business (T+6h to T+14h)**\n- **Services:** 30 Stateful Services with Relational Databases (Category 2)\n- **Goal:** Migrate core business logic services and their associated databases.\n- **Key Activities:** Execute pre-planned database migrations (e.g., AWS DMS to Cloud SQL). Replicate data and perform a coordinated cutover. Deploy corresponding services to Cloud Run and reconfigure them to connect to Cloud SQL.\n- **Success Criteria:** All 30 services and their databases are running on GCP. Data integrity is verified. End-to-end integration tests pass.\n\n--- \n\n**Wave 3: The Specialists (T+14h to T+20h)**\n- **Services:** 15 Stateful Services with NoSQL/Cache (Category 3)\n- **Goal:** Migrate services with specialized data store dependencies.\n- **Key Activities:** Execute migrations from DynamoDB/ElastiCache to Firestore/Memorystore. Deploy services and validate performance and data access patterns against the new data stores.\n- **Success Criteria:** All 15 services are running on GCP, meeting performance baselines. Caching and data access layers function correctly.\n\n--- \n\n**Wave 4: The Stragglers (T+20h to T+24h)**\n- **Services:** 5 Complex Legacy & High-Dependency Services (Category 4)\n- **Goal:** Migrate the highest-risk services once the rest of the ecosystem is stable on GCP.\n- **Key Activities:** Perform any required re-architecture or refactoring. Execute migration with heightened monitoring and a dedicated rollback plan. Perform extensive integration and regression testing.\n- **Success Criteria:** All 5 services are successfully migrated and fully functional. The entire 100-service ecosystem is stable and operational on GCP. Post-migration validation is complete.",
    "category": "strategy-document",
    "source": "Strategist",
    "tags": [
      "migration-plan",
      "wave-planning",
      "gcp-migration"
    ]
  }
}
```
